      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "5b0746088fa72313783e3269",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2023-12-12T20:37:01.218Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "6554345b10d692d6c08fbb6a",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "The Standard lite shader has this bug in worlds as well. I use it extensively with emissive maps, and as soon as you save your scene, all of the emissive surfaces go dark. \n\nYou have to go through all of the materials using \"standard lite\" (and having an emissive property set), one by one, and open the material dropdown , this brings back the emissivity.\n\nBut there is no way to upload a world correctly, because upon build the scene is saved and all of the emissive surfaces again go dark....This is a huge issue, completely breaks anything that uses standard lite with emissive...",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "6578c68c90472526a333a826",
      "aiGenerated": false,
      "author": {
        "_id": "61c1f170d585bf0b4b32bd64",
        "alias": "Sweet Reindeer",
        "aliasID": "65cabcfbed6eefce4cf48ad4",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/2f46060b7861a006d8a62cc8ca4221ce.png",
        "created": "2021-12-21T15:23:28.484Z",
        "name": "Equinn",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": null
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "5b0746088fa72313783e3269",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2023-12-12T20:46:04.658Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "6554345b10d692d6c08fbb6a",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "For a temporary fix, until they figure it out and release a patch, do the following to fix the emissive issue:\n\n1. Open the shader in visual studio (either by double clicking the shader (VRChat-Mobile-StandardLite) in the project browser or clicking \"edit\" in the material, next to the shader dropdown\n\n2. Find and comment out the line (should be line 18)[Toggle(_EMISSION)]_EnableEmission(\"Enable Emission\", int) = 0\"  \n\n3. Under \"CGPROGRAM\" (line 38) add a new line: #define _EMISSION 1\n\nIf this workaround causes a shader compile error, then just comment out line 46 (#pragma multi_compile _ _EMISSION)\n\nThis will permanently enable emissive on all materials using this shader. You wont see any changes on materials that use the standard lite shader if you didn't set an emissive value as it's 0 by default. This will also incur a (very) small performance hit as the shader will always do an extra multiplication for the emissive channel. But at least your stuff will work again.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
